body:
Suppose that you want to trace a process and that process launches a child process that uses a setuid binary.  Running with `strace` works because it doesn't trace the child processes.  Running with `strace -f` (perhaps to trace threads in the main process) will not work properly.  It will trace the child process and then the kernel will decide to not make the process setuid, which is likely to break the child process.

`strace -f -b execve` seems like it is designed for just this purpose, but it also fails.  The `execve` happens first, the kernel decides not to make the process setuid, and then strace detaches.  Here's a concrete example.  You'll need to provide some username in place of `$SOME_OTHER_USER`.  You can use `root` if you want to, but using a dummy user is probably more advisable.

```Shell
$ mkdir strace_setuid
$ cd strace_setuid
$ cat > mysetuid.c <<EOF
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    const int uid = getuid();
    const int euid = geteuid();
    if (euid == uid) {
        puts("setuid ignored");
        return 1;
    }
    puts("setuid working");
    return 0;
}
EOF

$ chmod go+w .
$ sudo -u $SOME_OTHER_USER sh -c 'gcc -Wall mysetuid.c -o mysetuid && chmod +s mysetuid'

# Verify that the program runs setuid when run directly
$ ./mysetuid
setuid working

# We can successfully trace a process that launches mysetuid as a subprocess
$ strace -o /dev/null sh -c './mysetuid; exit'
setuid working

# Using `-f` will fail, as expected
$ strace -o /dev/null -f sh -c './mysetuid; exit'
setuid ignored

# Using `-f -b execve` doesn't fix it
$ strace -o /dev/null -f -b execve sh -c './mysetuid; exit'
setuid ignored
```

