I assume that there must be a reason that things aren't already being done this way.  Maybe because this will detach even if the `execve` call fails?  The manual page doesn't really specify that strace only detaches on a successful `execve` and I guess from reading the manual I would have assumed that it detaches on any `execve`, not just successful ones.  That's not the current behavior, AFAICT.


Another alternative may be to add an option similar to valgrind's `--trace-children-skip=` (https://valgrind.org/docs/manual/manual-core.html#opt.trace-children-skip).  This would allow the explicit listing of any setuid processes that the user did not want to trace into.  It would be a bit inconvenient as it would be left to the user to discover all needed path(s) to specify.  But it would allow tracing of all other child processes, which could be useful.


I was going to suggest maybe looking at the binary to be executed and seeing if it was setuid and automatically detaching if it was.  Since filesystem capabilities are becoming more popular, just looking at the setuid bit probably isn't enough anymore.  Figuring out all the different things that might need to be checked could be complicated and maybe that's too much to expect strace to do.

